{"gaps": [
  {
    "what": "The adapter-to-transport-class matrix is not stated and is NOT derivable from the repo",
    "why_blocking": "The ticket requires all seven adapters to declare which of subscription/api/gateway they can serve and forbids declaring one they cannot, but no file in the repo records whether codex can be driven by OPENAI_API_KEY rather than its ChatGPT session, or whether pi/cursor/copilot can serve anything but a subscription — this is operator knowledge about live harness auth, and guessing it produces a registry that validates and then dispatches on the wrong credential, which the ticket itself calls worse than a load failure."
  },
  {
    "what": "Where a gateway transport's ENDPOINT comes from",
    "why_blocking": "opencode is a gateway harness and a gateway needs an endpoint as well as a key, but the ticket names only 'a named credential variable' and §4b forbids command/argv-shaped fields in the registry, so a fresh agent cannot tell whether the endpoint is adapter-owned, a registry field, or an env var it must not invent."
  },
  {
    "what": "The concrete shape of the adapter transport declaration",
    "why_blocking": "Criteria assert that 'the api-transport seat is given its declared credential variable', but the declaration's data shape is only described by example, so two implementers would produce two incompatible schemas and the validator in @adlc/quartermaster has to agree with it byte-for-byte across a package boundary."
  },
  {
    "what": "What marks a subscription seat as materially distinct once HOME redirection is ruled out",
    "why_blocking": "With HOME deliberately untouched, the only remaining distinction is the presence or absence of the api credential variable; the ticket should say so explicitly, because an implementer reading 'the subscription seat retains its session context' may believe some positive session marker is required and invent one."
  }
]}
